{
  "Comment": "Data-quality control plane (SPEC-108 skeleton — the canonical state list; later specs WIRE these states, none adds one). validate-authorize -> inspect-source -> create-temp-tables -> start-profile-query -> [WaitForQuery -> CheckQuery -> Choice] -> byte-scan -> assemble-report -> drop-temp-tables -> RecordSuccess. TWO structural decisions live here and must not be retrofitted: (1) THE ATHENA POLL IS IN THIS STATE MACHINE, not inside a Lambda; (2) every Catch reaches DropTempTablesOnFailure BEFORE Fail. Each Task threads the running request object via Payload.$=$ + OutputPath=$.Payload.",
  "StartAt": "ValidateAuthorize",
  "States": {
    "ValidateAuthorize": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "First step. The Pipe forwards the raw SQS batch (a JSON ARRAY), so Payload.$=$ hands the array to the validator (it reads record[0]); OutputPath=$.Payload makes the validated request object the whole state. A failure here runs BEFORE the request identity exists, so its Catch (no ResultPath — the input is an array, not an object) routes to ValidateFailureSeed, which seeds null identity fields so the shared failure path is uniform. Wired real in SPEC-109.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${validate_authorize_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "Next": "ValidateFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "InspectSource": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "List + classify by extension, probe every file's header, write the S3 MANIFEST and pass its key onward. States pass a manifest POINTER, never an inline file list: Step Functions caps a state payload at 256 KB and States.DataLimitExceeded is thrown OUTSIDE any Catch — so an inline list would give the user NO notification at all, the worst failure mode in the pipeline. Wired in SPEC-110.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${inspect_source_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "CreateTempTables"
    },
    "CreateTempTables": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Create the temp external table over the SOURCE (+ for CSV, the single-column RAW-LINE table the ragged check needs). Temp tables live in the USER'S OWN database, which is exactly why every failure path below must reach DropTempTables. Wired in SPEC-111.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${create_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "StartProfileQuery"
    },
    "StartProfileQuery": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "START the Athena profile query and return its QueryExecutionId — it does NOT wait. StartQueryExecution carries a ClientRequestToken (= requestId), or a Retry after a post-submission transient error would start a SECOND query. Wired in SPEC-111.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${start_profile_query_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "WaitForQuery"
    },
    "WaitForQuery": {
      "Type": "Wait",
      "Comment": "THE POLL LIVES HERE, IN THE STATE MACHINE — not inside a Lambda. table-builder's execute-ddl polls Athena inside a 300 s Lambda, which is fine for a CREATE TABLE that returns in seconds and FATAL for a profiling query over 250 GB that runs for minutes to tens of minutes: Lambda's hard ceiling is 900 s. A Standard Step Functions execution runs for up to a year, so waiting here removes the time ceiling entirely. This is the whole reason the arc works; do not move this poll into a Lambda.",
      "Seconds": 30,
      "Next": "CheckQuery"
    },
    "CheckQuery": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "One GetQueryExecution. Returns the request object plus queryStatus. A FAILED/CANCELLED query raises TerminalError inside the verb (SPEC-111), so the Choice below only ever sees QUEUED / RUNNING / SUCCEEDED.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${check_query_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "QueryComplete"
    },
    "QueryComplete": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "Loop back to WaitForQuery while the query is still QUEUED/RUNNING; otherwise (SUCCEEDED) proceed. There is no unbounded-loop risk: the account's DML query-timeout quota terminates a runaway query, which CheckQuery surfaces as a terminal error.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "Variable": "$.queryStatus",
          "StringEquals": "QUEUED",
          "Next": "WaitForQuery"
        },
        {
          "Variable": "$.queryStatus",
          "StringEquals": "RUNNING",
          "Next": "WaitForQuery"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "ByteScan"
    },
    "ByteScan": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "The ONE thing SQL cannot see: a bounded, SAMPLED raw-byte encoding check. Its budget is PER REQUEST (DQ_SCAN_TOTAL_BYTES, default 2 GiB), divided across the manifest's files — never per file. Wired in SPEC-112.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${byte_scan_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "AssembleReport"
    },
    "AssembleReport": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Join the Athena results + the byte scan + the cross-file column agreement (computed from inspect-source's per-file probe, NOT in SQL — an external table has one schema for the whole folder and would HIDE exactly that disagreement) into report.json. Wired in SPEC-112.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${assemble_report_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "DropTempTables"
    },
    "DropTempTables": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Success-path cleanup. Idempotent by design — it must tolerate being reached before any table was created. Touches GLUE ONLY (no s3:DeleteObject is granted anywhere in this arc), which is what makes it safe to reach from every path. Wired in SPEC-111.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${drop_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "ParseError"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordSuccess"
    },
    "RecordSuccess": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Shape the terminal SUCCESS outcome SPEC-114 notifies from. SPEC-112 fills in the verdict/summary fields. Nothing durable is written by a terminal state — the report object is the artefact. THE IDENTITY CONTRACT: $.requestId and $.actor must EXIST as keys by here — ValidateAuthorize guarantees it. A Pass state's JSONPath HARD-FAILS on an ABSENT key (States.Runtime) and a Pass state has NO Catch, so that failure escapes straight to ExecutionFailed, skipping NotifyFailure and leaving the user with no notification at all. A null VALUE is fine; a missing key is fatal. (Caught by the SPEC-108 live smoke test.)",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "status": "completed"
      },
      "Next": "Succeeded"
    },
    "ValidateFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "A validate-authorize failure occurs before the request identity exists (its input was the raw SQS array), so its Catch replaced the state with the error object. Seed null identity fields + carry the error under $.error so the shared ParseError/cleanup/RecordFailure path is uniform for every verb.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId": null,
        "actor": null,
        "error.$": "$"
      },
      "Next": "ParseError"
    },
    "ParseError": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Decode the Lambda error Cause (a JSON string) so RecordFailure can read the errorMessage — the 'reason: detail' string TerminalError raised.",
      "Parameters": {
        "cause.$": "States.StringToJson($.error.Cause)"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.parsed",
      "Next": "DropTempTablesOnFailure"
    },
    "DropTempTablesOnFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "THE CATCH ALWAYS REACHES CLEANUP. The failure-path twin of DropTempTables, invoking the SAME Lambda (a state can have only one Next, hence the twin). Without it, every failed request would LEAK a temp table into the user's own Glue database — visible in the Query workspace's schema rail and eventually colliding with their real table names. ResultPath parks the result on a scratch field so $.parsed (the decoded error) survives for RecordFailure. Its OWN Catch goes straight to RecordFailure: cleanup is best-effort on the failure path and must NEVER mask the original error, and this is what stops the failure path looping back into itself.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${drop_temp_tables_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.cleanup",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.cleanupError",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RecordFailure": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The single recorded-failure state every verb's Catch reaches (after cleanup). Shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, status:'failed', reason } (identity is null for a pre-identity validate failure). SPEC-112 shapes it further; SPEC-114 notifies from it.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "status": "failed",
        "reason.$": "$.parsed.cause.errorMessage"
      },
      "Next": "NotifyFailure"
    },
    "NotifyFailure": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Emit the friendly failure notification to the actor. PLACEHOLDER here — SPEC-114 FR-004 wires the real handler. Its Catch goes straight to Failed: a missed notification must never change the recorded outcome, and the execution has already failed.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${notify_failure_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.notified",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.notifyError",
          "Next": "Failed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "Failed"
    },
    "Succeeded": {
      "Type": "Succeed"
    },
    "Failed": {
      "Type": "Fail",
      "Error": "DataQualityCheckFailed",
      "Cause": "The data-quality control plane recorded an unrecoverable error (see the RecordFailure state output for the structured { status:'failed', reason } outcome)."
    }
  }
}
